<PageTitle>FlexCore FAQ: .NET Support, Grid, Reports, Comparisons</PageTitle>

<div class="docs-page">
    <h1>Frequently asked questions</h1>
    <p>
        Short answers to what developers ask before they pick a Blazor component library. Each one can be checked against the code or
        the page it links to. If your question is not here, <a href="signup">ask it</a>; a person answers.
    </p>

    <section>
        <h2>Compatibility</h2>

        <h3 id="dotnet-versions">Which .NET versions does FlexCore support?</h3>
        <p>.NET 10: both packages target <code>net10.0</code>. Older frameworks are not supported today.</p>

        <h3 id="render-modes">Does it work with Blazor Server, WebAssembly, Auto render mode, static server rendering and MAUI Hybrid?</h3>
        <p>
            Blazor Server with interactive rendering is what this site runs and what has been verified. The controls need an interactive render
            mode, because clicks, keys and drags travel through Blazor events; under static server rendering they draw but do not react.
            WebAssembly, Auto and MAUI Hybrid have not been verified yet; FlexCore references <code>Microsoft.Data.Sqlite</code>, so a
            WebAssembly host would need testing before you rely on it.
        </p>

        <h3 id="javascript">Does FlexCore use JavaScript, npm or a CSS framework?</h3>
        <p>
            The controls render HTML and inline SVG from C#. A few ship a small script of their own inside the package: the dialog, the
            splitter, the rich text editor, the text box's context menu, the grid's scroll-position reader, and the PDF viewer's bundled
            pdf.js. There is no npm package, no bundler step and nothing fetched from a CDN. Styling is the library's own <code>fx-</code>
            classes and CSS variables; it does not depend on Bootstrap or Tailwind and defines no global rules, so it sits beside whatever
            stylesheet your application already has. Icons are the class names you pass in; this site uses Bootstrap Icons.
        </p>

        <h3 id="alongside">Can I use FlexCore next to MudBlazor, Radzen or Telerik, or migrate one page at a time?</h3>
        <p>
            Yes. Everything lives in the <code>Fx.ControlKit</code> namespaces and under the <code>fx-</code> CSS prefix, with no global
            resets, so a page can mix libraries while you move it over.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>Capabilities</h2>

        <h3 id="how-many">How many controls are there, and which ones?</h3>
        <p>
            130-plus standalone controls (137 at the October 2026 count). <a href="components">All components</a> lists them in 13 groups,
            each with a live demo and the code behind it.
        </p>

        <h3 id="large-data">Can the data grid handle large data sets?</h3>
        <p>
            Row windowing renders only the rows in view (<a href="demo/grid/row-selection-50k">100,000 generated rows</a> on this site),
            column virtualization does the same for <a href="demo/grid/column-virtualization">wide grids</a>, and an <code>ItemsProvider</code>
            lets your database serve rows, groups, totals and export pages <a href="demo/grid/provider-enterprise">as they are needed</a>.
            <a href="demo/grid/enterprise-suite">Exports</a> to CSV, .xlsx and PDF take the rows as filtered and sorted.
        </p>

        <h3 id="theming">Can I theme it to match my brand? Dark mode? Right-to-left?</h3>
        <p>
            Each control reads <code>--fx-*</code> CSS variables for its colours, borders and sizes (the CSS variables table on its page lists
            them), and <code>--fx-zoom</code> scales every control together. There is no built-in dark theme and no right-to-left layout
            today. Built-in labels are English; numbers and dates follow .NET format strings and the current culture.
        </p>

        <h3 id="accessibility">Are the controls accessible?</h3>
        <p>
            Most demo pages have a <em>Keyboard and accessibility</em> section that states exactly what the control does and does not do:
            roles, keys, focus order and the gaps. There is no WCAG conformance claim and no VPAT yet.
        </p>

        <h3 id="crystal-reports">Does the report writer run my Crystal Reports?</h3>
        <p>
            <a href="demo/reports/package-costs">ReportWriterControl</a> loads a Crystal Reports XML export, with its tables, joins, groups,
            formulas and field placement, and renders it as paginated pages in the browser with a group tree, search, print and HTML, CSV and
            Excel export; the data comes from an <code>IReportDataExecutor</code> you register. Native <code>.rpt</code> files need an
            <code>IReportExporter</code> supplied by the host.
        </p>

        <h3 id="migration">I am moving a VB6 or WinForms application to the web. What is there for me?</h3>
        <p>
            MDI windows, a <a href="demo/tree/treeview-explorer">tree view</a> with the classic VB6 connector lines, Win32-style
            <a href="demo/buttons">buttons</a> and <a href="demo/dialog">dialogs</a> with a system menu, a
            <a href="demo/page-navigation">keyboard navigation graph</a> for forms worked without a mouse, and the report writer for
            Crystal Reports.
        </p>

        <h3 id="ai-assistants">Does FlexCore work with AI coding assistants?</h3>
        <p>
            There is no MCP server yet. The documentation is published in the forms agents read well: <a href="llms.txt">/llms.txt</a>,
            <a href="llms-full.txt">/llms-full.txt</a> and a Markdown twin of every page (add <code>.md</code> to its address), and every
            code sample on this site starts with the <code>@@using</code> lines it needs, so it compiles when pasted.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>Support and project</h2>

        <h3 id="help">Where do I get help, and how quickly?</h3>
        <p>
            Bugs and feature requests go to <a href="https://github.com/wadoodachaudhary/FlexCore/issues" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub issues</a>;
            questions go through the <a href="signup">sign-up form</a> and are answered by a person, usually within two working days.
        </p>

        <h3 id="releases">How often does FlexCore release, and how stable is the API?</h3>
        <p>
            48 releases on NuGet between May and September 2026 (0.1.0 to 0.2.47); the demos here run 0.2.49. The version is still 0.x: new
            parameters arrive most releases, and each page's documentation tables say which version they were checked against.
        </p>

        <h3 id="contribute">How do I request a feature or contribute?</h3>
        <p>Open an issue on GitHub, or a pull request.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>Comparison</h2>

        <h3 id="versus">How is FlexCore different from MudBlazor, Radzen, Telerik or Syncfusion?</h3>
        <p>
            MudBlazor is a Material Design component library; Radzen Blazor is an MIT component set paired with the paid Radzen Blazor
            Studio; Telerik UI for Blazor, Syncfusion and DevExpress are commercial suites licensed per developer. FlexCore is a library
            built around data-heavy line-of-business screens (data grid, tree grid, pivot table, report writer, scheduler, MDI) and around
            moving VB6 and WinForms applications to the web, rendered from C# and SVG with no npm step.
        </p>

        <h3 id="quickgrid">How is the data grid different from QuickGrid?</h3>
        <p>
            QuickGrid, Microsoft's lightweight grid, covers sorting, paging and virtualization. <a href="demo/grid">GridControl</a> adds
            filtering with Excel-style menus, grouping with aggregates, inline, dialog and batch editing, frozen columns, column
            virtualization, provider-fed loading and Excel, CSV and PDF export.
        </p>

        <h3 id="start">How do I get started?</h3>
        <p>
            Install the package, add the namespaces, register two services and put a grid on a page: <a href="get-started">Get Started</a>
            walks through it with a complete page.
        </p>
    </section>
</div>
